# Tor hidden service keys

Tor creates one subdirectory per onion service here, each holding that
service's private key and its generated `hostname`. Tor owns those
subdirectories: it makes them mode 0700 and refuses to start if it finds them
more permissive than that.

This README exists so the directory itself ships in the embedded assets and is
created by `homelab setup`, owned by you. Without it the directory does not
exist when the tor container first starts, Docker creates the bind-mount target
as root:root, and then neither you nor tor (which runs as your uid inside the
container) can create anything in it — `homelab enable <svc> --tor` fails with
"permission denied".

If that already happened, the fix is one command:

    sudo chown -R "$(id -u):$(id -g)" ~/.config/homelab/tor/hidden_service

Never delete this directory: the keys in it *are* your .onion addresses.
